# <%= name %> — Server Configuration

## Files

- `nginx.conf` — Nginx configuration for containerised / reverse-proxy deployments
- `environments/<%= name %>/htaccess.<env>` (workspace root) — Apache configuration for
  shared-hosting or cPanel deployments, one file per environment

## Nginx (Docker / Kubernetes)

Copy `nginx.conf` into your Docker image:

```dockerfile
COPY dist/<%= root %>/ /usr/share/nginx/html/
COPY <%= root %>/server/nginx.conf /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf
```

## Apache

Release builds (`nx run <%= name %>:release:<env>`) copy that env's `htaccess.<env>` into the
build output as `.htaccess`; plain `nx build` adds none. To build without one, set
`"htaccess": false` on the release target. A new environment (`pstack env --name=<env>`) gets
a copy of `htaccess.prod`. The `.htaccess` needs the
`mod_rewrite`, `mod_headers`, `mod_include`, `mod_unique_id` and `mod_setenvif` Apache
modules (the last three give each request its own CSP nonce when the release build runs with
`secure: true`), and `AllowOverride FileInfo Options=IncludesNOEXEC` (or `All`) for the
folder. When the server sets its own nonce variable (e.g. `UNIQUE_ID` from mod_lua), run
`pstack csp --app=<%= name %> --nonce-source=env:UNIQUE_ID`: mod_unique_id and mod_setenvif are
then not needed.

## CSP overrides

Per-app Content Security Policy additions (e.g. API domains for `connect-src`) live
in `<%= root %>/csp-allowlist.json`. Change it with
`pstack csp --app=<%= name %> --add=connect-src:https://api.example.com` (or edit it and run
`pstack csp --app=<%= name %>`); that rewrites every env's `htaccess.<env>` and `nginx.conf`. Files without the
"Generated by pstack csp" first line are kept unless `--force`.
